[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] The Jeonju&ndash;Wanju integration debate has reignited, but with concerns over welfare cuts and debt burdens deepening public distrust, restoring political trust has emerged as the top priority. Photo shows a banner at Samnye Market. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, Sept. 5, 2025.



[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] The debate over the integration of Jeonju and Wanju has reignited, putting Jeonbuk Province and Jeonju City&rsquo;s ability to manage and persuade on trial. Although Jeonbuk Province and Jeonju City accepted the &ldquo;105 coexistence development plans&rdquo; proposed by the integration promotion group and promised to push for legislation, distrust over reduced benefits and avoidance of responsibility remains strong on the ground. Resident interviews and recent local opinion polls reveal both divided public sentiment and conflicting evaluations of leadership.

◆&ldquo;Will welfare shrink, and only debt remain?&rdquo;

Many Wanju residents fear integration could bring more losses than gains. A strong perception persists that maintaining the current system is preferable, given concerns they may shoulder Jeonju City&rsquo;s financial debt burden.

Song Jeom-rye (alias, 77), who runs a clothing and bedding shop in Bongdong, said, &ldquo;Wanju has many benefits. There&rsquo;s a lot of support for childbirth, and flood damage is less frequent, so it&rsquo;s a good place to live. That&rsquo;s why people can&rsquo;t help but dislike integration.&rdquo; She added, &ldquo;For Bongdong&rsquo;s development, integration may be necessary, but in truth, I&rsquo;d rather it not happen. Most of my customers say they don&rsquo;t want it either.&rdquo;

Kim Gap-san (alias, in his 70s), who operates a clothing store, said, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s true that someday, for the sake of Jeonbuk, we&rsquo;ll need to integrate.&rdquo; However, he added, &ldquo;People worry Jeonju has too much debt. Many fear they&rsquo;ll lose current benefits if integration happens.&rdquo;

A pesticide shop owner in Samnye voiced opposition tied to welfare and finances: &ldquo;Since the COVID days, Jeonju hasn&rsquo;t given a single penny in support, but Wanju has provided solid aid. They gave 300,000 won before, and now there&rsquo;s talk of another 1 million won per person. If we merge, that won&rsquo;t continue,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Wanju has no debt, but Jeonju is drowning in it. They&rsquo;ll use Wanju&rsquo;s money to pay off Jeonju&rsquo;s debt. Ninety percent of Wanju residents oppose this,&rdquo; he asserted.


