The Jeonju&ndash;Wanju integration debate has reignited, but with concerns over welfare cuts and debt burdens deepening public distrust, restoring political trust has emerged as the top priority. Photo shows Jeonju Mayor Woo Beom-gi visiting Yongjeon-myeon, Wanju County, on September 1 for a luncheon meeting with the Jeonju&ndash;Wanju Integration Promotion Committee. (Provided by Jeonju City Hall) ⓒCheonji Ilbo, Sept. 5, 2025.



◆Expectations for growth vs fears of marginalization

Residents&rsquo; views on integration diverged along lifestyle and economic interests. Some in commercial districts supported integration, citing growth prospects tied to Jeonju, while others opposed it, citing fears of being relegated to the periphery or hosting unwanted facilities.

Park Jang-il (alias, 72), who runs an electronics shop in Samnye, expressed support from a business perspective: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m in favor. In Samnye, most shopkeepers are in favor,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;All the students go to schools in Jeonju. Parents commute from Samnye to Jeonju for school districts, so it only makes sense to integrate.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;Jeonju is hemmed in by Wanju and can&rsquo;t expand. For integration, Wanju also needs a plus. It shouldn&rsquo;t be unconditional integration.&rdquo; He further noted, &ldquo;Opponents seem to be blocking supporters from speaking up.&rdquo;

Song Kang-hyeok (alias, 39), who operates a clothing store in Samnye, supported integration from a long-term perspective: &ldquo;In the long run, integration is right. This isn&rsquo;t purely a Wanju market&mdash;many Jeonju customers come here too,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;At first, support for integration was stronger, but recently opposition has grown. I saw leaflets being handed out in Bongdong urging people to oppose. Residents need to be persuaded.&rdquo;

Kwon Geum-nae (alias, 69), who runs a repair shop, voiced concerns about marginalization and development priorities: &ldquo;Since Wanju surrounds Jeonju, integration makes sense for Jeonju. But Jeonju has only expanded toward Hyocheon-dong and Seosin-dong. If we integrate now, Wanju will just become the outskirts,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Before, people clamored to move into Bongdong&rsquo;s Coaru apartments, but now with new housing in Songcheon-dong, prices have dropped. After Iksan integrated, Hamyeol declined.&rdquo;

She added, &ldquo;Wanju is mountainous. Will they just build more housing? Eventually they&rsquo;ll dump unwanted facilities here. They talked about integration 50 years ago, but now, when Jeonju has already expanded, rushing this feels wrong.&rdquo;

◆Coexistence pledges, but distrust persists

Jeonbuk Province and Jeonju City promised to legislate the 105 coexistence development plans proposed by the integration group. These included: a ban on relocating unwanted facilities without Wanju residents&rsquo; consent; maintaining and expanding Wanju residents&rsquo; benefits for more than 12 years; guaranteeing at least 11 Wanju district council seats for 12 years; and building the new integrated city hall and council chamber in Wanju.

Yet distrust remains. Opinion polls confirmed the opposition trend. A Daily Research poll (commissioned by Wanju Shinmun) conducted on August 1&ndash;2 found 71.0% opposed and 25.9% in favor. Another Korea Information Research poll (commissioned by K Journal) on July 23&ndash;24 showed 65.0% opposed and 30.7% in favor among Wanju residents.


